\noindent\fbox{\parbox{0.97\textwidth}{\small\textbf{The core problem.} Anyone who uses V\'elib' in Paris knows the frustration of reaching a station that is empty when you need a bike, or full when you need to return one. We forecast whether a station will become \textit{critical} (nearly empty or nearly full) within the next \textbf{15 minutes}, and test whether knowing what surrounds the station (universities, business districts, tourist landmarks) makes that forecast noticeably more accurate.}}

\section{1. Research Questions}

We break the project into three core research questions that build sequentially upon one another:
\begin{itemize}[itemsep=2pt, topsep=2pt]
    \item \textbf{Q1 (Urban Context):} How does station behavior and turnover change with geographic and functional location? Do stations near universities, office districts, tourist landmarks, or steep topography (e.g., Montmartre) exhibit distinct daily occupancy profiles?
    \item \textbf{Q2 (Temporal and Meteorological Factors):} How do diurnal commuting patterns, rush hours, weekdays versus weekends, precipitation, temperature variations, and wind conditions alter bike borrowing and return rates?
    \item \textbf{Q3 (Criticality Prediction):} Can machine learning reliably predict station criticality 15 minutes ahead, and does augmenting the feature space with spatial/urban context scores provide a measurable lift over purely autoregressive and meteorological signals?
\end{itemize}

\section{2. Data Sources and Collection}

The V\'elib' system updates dynamically and offers no historical dock status archive, necessitating a continuous ingestion pipeline. A scheduled cron job running on \textbf{Railway} queries the official V\'elib' GBFS status API every 15 minutes across a 30-day continuous collection window. Snapshots are partitioned and serialized directly into \textbf{Parquet} format to ensure columnar compression and high-throughput query performance. Static station layout metadata is queried once, as geographic coordinates and physical dock capacities remain stable.

Historical meteorological measurements are extracted retroactively via the \textbf{Open-Meteo} Historical Weather API for Paris coordinates (48.8566$^\circ$N, 2.3522$^\circ$E). Because Open-Meteo archives granular surface-level observations, a single deterministic extract suffices and is joined onto the V\'elib' status records by timestamp alignment.

The resulting dataset comprises three distinct components:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Status Time Series:} $\approx 1{,}510\text{ stations} \times 4\text{ reads/hr} \times 24\text{ hrs} \times 30\text{ days} \approx \mathbf{4.35\text{ million rows}}$.
    \item \textbf{Station Metadata Table:} $1{,}510\text{ unique station rows}$ detailing physical capacity and coordinates.
    \item \textbf{Weather Matrix:} $720\text{ hourly intervals}$ ($30\text{ days} \times 24\text{ h}$) joined across stations, accompanied by 30 day-level environmental aggregates.
\end{itemize}

